            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes an array of random values from the normal distribution - the bell curve. Most values land near the mean; fewer and fewer further out. About two thirds fall within one spread of the mean, and 95 in a hundred within two.\n\nIt is the shape of natural variation: measurement noise, small human imprecision, the scatter of many small independent causes added together. Use it to add believable jitter to a movement, a pitch or a timing; to simulate sensor noise for testing a filter; or to scatter points around a centre.\n\nnp.rand gives evenly spread values instead, every value in its range equally likely.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.random.normal <dims ...>\nDefault: 10 values.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.random.normal 100\nnp.random.normal 24 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes a new array and sends it.\n\nshape (text box, also an inlet):\nThe size along each axis: 100 for a list of 100, 24 3 for 24 rows of 3.\n\nmean (float box, also an inlet):\nThe centre of the curve.\n\nspread (float box, also an inlet):\nThe standard deviation: how wide the curve is. 0 gives the mean every time.\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) draws different numbers every time the patch opens. Any other number starts the same sequence every time - the same \"random\" piece on every run, which is what you want for rehearsal, comparison and debugging.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom array:\nThe values (32-bit floats).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2000 values five times a second, counted into 24 bins from -3 to 3: the bell shape, tallest at 0, nearly empty beyond 2 either way. Change spread to 0.5 and it narrows;\nchange mean and it slides along.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.rand gives evenly spread values. np.random.integers gives whole numbers. random.gauss gives a single value; the t.dist nodes give PyTorch tensors from many distributions.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1100,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes an array of random values from the normal distribution - the bell curve. Most values land near the mean; fewer and fewer further out. About two thirds fall within one spread of the mean, and 95 in a hundred within two.\n\nIt is the shape of natural variation: measurement noise, small human imprecision, the scatter of many small independent causes added together. Use it to add believable jitter to a movement, a pitch or a timing; to simulate sensor noise for testing a filter; or to scatter points around a centre.\n\nnp.rand gives evenly spread values instead, every value in its range equally likely.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.random.normal <dims ...>\nDefault: 10 values.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.random.normal 100\nnp.random.normal 24 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes a new array and sends it.\n\nshape (text box, also an inlet):\nThe size along each axis: 100 for a list of 100, 24 3 for 24 rows of 3.\n\nmean (float box, also an inlet):\nThe centre of the curve.\n\nspread (float box, also an inlet):\nThe standard deviation: how wide the curve is. 0 gives the mean every time.\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) draws different numbers every time the patch opens. Any other number starts the same sequence every time - the same \"random\" piece on every run, which is what you want for rehearsal, comparison and debugging.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom array:\nThe values (32-bit floats).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2000 values five times a second, counted into 24 bins from -3 to 3: the bell shape, tallest at 0, nearly empty beyond 2 either way. Change spread to 0.5 and it narrows;\nchange mean and it slides along.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.rand gives evenly spread values. np.random.integers gives whole numbers. random.gauss gives a single value; the t.dist nodes give PyTorch tensors from many distributions."
        },
